      "Each eye computes its own vector to the pointer from its own centre — not one shared vector — so the two pupils converge slightly on a nearby cursor, which is what makes it read as <i>looking at</i> rather than <i>sliding with</i>. Frame-diffing the source: both pupils sit hard against the same edge of the socket whenever the cursor is far, and cross when it is between them.",
      "The vector is divided by a reach of 260px and clamped to the unit disc, then written as two custom properties; CSS turns them into <code>translate(calc(var(--px) * 14px), calc(var(--py) * 12px))</code>. The clamp is what keeps the pupil inside the white — without it a far cursor drags it out through the lid.",
      "Three layers of lag: pupils 160ms, brows 320ms, head 700ms, all the same ease-out. The eyes snap, the face follows. One transition speed for everything reads as a rigid mask sliding.",
      "Brows do not translate — they <b>rotate</b> ±6° with <code>--px</code> and lift 3px with <code>--py</code>. Looking up raises the brows; looking sideways tilts them. Both are the same two numbers the pupils use.",
      "The blink is the socket's own colour sliding down as a lid: a 180ms keyframe on <code>translateY</code>, both eyes on the same timer, every 3–6 seconds. Restarting it is <code>classList.remove → void offsetWidth → classList.add</code>, the standard replay-a-CSS-animation dance.",
      "Fur is an SVG <code>feTurbulence + feDisplacementMap</code> filter (frequency .9, scale 9) applied to three plain rounded shapes. It costs one filter pass on layout, and nothing on pointer move, because the shapes never change — only the small elements inside them transform.",
      "When the pointer leaves the document (<code>mouseout</code> with <code>relatedTarget === null</code>) the creature looks back at the reader: the vector is reset to the page centre rather than left wherever the cursor was last seen.",
